<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; max-width: 900px; margin: auto; border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; background-color: #f8f8f8;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Historical Sites – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic; color: #333;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Historical Sites and Tourism</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Our Historical Sites</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Narrate both fiction and non-fiction stories. Interactive: Conduct interviews on personal interests.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can narrate a story about an Indigenous festival in Panama's comarcas, including sensory details (e.g., "At the Ngäbe-Buglé celebration, we saw woven chaquiras, heard traditional flute music, and watched the dancers perform their ancestral steps." "During the Guna Yala celebration, we admired the vibrant molas with intricate patterns and listened to chants while the community shared stories of their ancestors." "At the Emberá cultural festival, we saw beautifully painted jaguá designs on the dancers' skin, heard the rhythmic beat of the drums, and tasted freshly cooked potatoes.").<br>Can conduct a brief interview about a peer's cultural background (e.g., "What traditions does your family celebrate?").<br>Can describe the history of an indigenous group in class discussions (e.g., "I'm interested in learning about the Guna people's history").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e0eaf3;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of famous Panamanian historical sites (e.g., Panama Canal, Casco Viejo, Panama Viejo ruins, forts). Students work in pairs to quickly name the sites and share one thing they remember about each. Teacher encourages them to use vocabulary like "historical sites," "ruins," "landmarks," "ancient," and "preserved."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models describing a personal experience at a historical site. "Last year, I visited Casco Viejo. It's a very attractive historical site with beautiful architecture. I walked through the narrow streets and explored some small museums. I am going to visit the Causeway next month with my family." The teacher explicitly uses present simple, simple past, and "going to," along with target vocabulary. The teacher also models the pronunciation of minimal pairs like /ɪ/ in "sit" and /iː/ in "seat" (e.g., "visit" vs "deep") and stress in words like "history," "historical," and "information."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is 'ancient,' is it new or very old?" (Expected answer: Very old)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'preserve' mean to keep something safe or to destroy it?" (Expected answer: To keep safe)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Historical Sites and Tourism".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio recording (1-2 minutes) of two Panamanian teenagers discussing their recent visits to an indigenous cultural festival and their plans to visit a historical landmark. The dialogue includes sensory details and questions about cultural background.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Participate in conversations about travel plans and historical knowledge, providing relevant descriptions and responses; Conduct interviews on personal interests.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (oral answers):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where did the first speaker go last weekend, and what did they see/hear/taste?" (Gist)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What historical site is the second speaker planning to visit, and why?" (Specific information)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Based on their tone, do they sound excited about their experiences and plans?" (Pragmatic: Understanding intent and context)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present simple and simple past for describing places and their importance; Future tense with "going to" for plans; Possessive forms with 's.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> historical sites, ruins, Panama Canal, Casco Viejo, landmarks, architecture, heritage, monument, destination, visitor, museum, tour, Causeway, colony, port, fort; attract, visit, guide, find, explore, travel, preserve, describe, inform, learn, reach, locate, transit, turn, walk; historical, cultural, famous, ancient, preserved, tourist-friendly, informative, renowned.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Minimal pairs in long and short vowels (e.g., /ɪ/ in "sit" and /iː/ in "seat"); Stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "culture," "cultural," "history," "historical").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion (5 min):</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board or projector, and students complete them orally with appropriate vocabulary and grammar. For example: "The Panama Canal ____ (attract) many ____ (visitor) every year." or "Casco Viejo ____ (be) once a vibrant ____ (colony)." Students take turns completing sentences, focusing on correct verb forms and word choice. Teacher listens for pronunciation of target vocabulary and stress.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice - Guided Dialogue (10 min):</b> In pairs, students practice short dialogues using sentence frames. Student A asks about a past visit, Student B responds using simple past and descriptive adjectives, then asks about future plans.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A: "What historical site did you <span style="text-decoration: underline;">visit</span> recently?" (Teacher emphasizes /ɪ/ in visit)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">B: "I visited <span style="text-decoration: underline;">Panama's</span> Casco Viejo last year. It was a very <span style="text-decoration: underline;">cultural</span> and <span style="text-decoration: underline;">informative</span> destination." (Teacher emphasizes possessive 's, stress on 'cultural', 'informative')</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">B: "What historical site are you <span style="text-decoration: underline;">going to</span> explore soon?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A: "I'm going to <span style="text-decoration: underline;">explore</span> the Guna Yala islands. I want to <span style="text-decoration: underline;">learn</span> about their <span style="text-decoration: underline;">heritage</span>."</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher circulates, providing feedback on grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - "Describe and Ask" (10 min):</b> Students choose two pictures of historical sites or indigenous festivals (provided by teacher or from their own knowledge). In pairs, they take turns describing one picture using sensory details (e.g., "I saw...", "I heard...", "I smelled...") and then asking their partner about their interest or future plans related to it. For example: "At the Emberá cultural festival, I saw beautiful jaguá designs on their skin, and I heard rhythmic drum beats. Are you going to visit an indigenous community soon? What traditions does your family celebrate?" This practices narrating stories and conducting interviews.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Historical Sites and Tourism".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "Cultural Explorer Interview"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in new pairs. One student takes on the role of an "Indigenous Culture Expert" or "Historical Site Enthusiast" and the other as a "Travel Blogger/Interviewer." The "Interviewer" asks questions about the "Expert's" experiences at indigenous festivals or historical sites, their cultural background, or future travel plans. The "Expert" narrates a story about a festival or describes the history of an indigenous group or a historical site, incorporating sensory details, historical facts, and plans. They must use present simple, simple past, "going to," possessive 's, and target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Interview Questions for Travel Blogger:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you tell me about a memorable indigenous festival you've attended? What did you see, hear, or taste?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What traditions does your family celebrate that connect to Panama's cultural heritage?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is <span style="text-decoration: underline;">Panama's</span> Casco Viejo such an attractive <span style="text-decoration: underline;">destination</span> for <span style="text-decoration: underline;">visitors</span>?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are you <span style="text-decoration: underline;">going to</span> visit any more <span style="text-decoration: underline;">historical sites</span> this year?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students swap roles after 3-4 minutes. The teacher monitors for fluency, interaction, and appropriate use of language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment: Observation Checklist / Peer Assessment</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher uses an observation checklist to note student participation, use of target language, and fluency during the "Cultural Explorer Interview." Alternatively, students can use a simple peer assessment checklist to evaluate their partner's performance (e.g., "Did my partner use past tense to narrate a story?", "Did they ask questions about traditions?", "Was their pronunciation clear?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' "Cultural Explorer Interview" performance from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially minimal pairs and stress in multisyllabic words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (nouns, verbs, adjectives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of present simple, simple past, "going to," and possessive 's.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence in narrating stories and answering questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective collaborative interaction and turn-taking.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new vocabulary word I feel confident using in a conversation is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Something I found challenging about speaking in English today was..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection, connection to next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well do I feel I can describe a historical site or a cultural event now?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of my speaking (vocabulary, grammar, pronunciation, fluency) do I want to improve next?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson, especially regarding student engagement in speaking activities? The pair work for the interview was very engaging, and students actively used the past tense and "going to" for their stories and plans. The focus on sensory details helped them narrate more vividly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Writing Composition)? Some students still hesitated with the correct use of possessive 's and sentence structure in complex descriptions. For the writing lesson, providing sentence starters or guided paragraph frames will help consolidate these grammatical structures. I need to ensure the writing task explicitly asks for descriptive paragraphs including sensory details.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build upon the oral narratives and descriptions practiced today. Students will use the same vocabulary and grammatical structures (present simple, simple past, "going to," possessive 's) to write descriptive paragraphs about a historical site or an indigenous festival, consolidating their productive skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>